.TH ZERODDS-IDLC 1 "" "ZeroDDS" "ZeroDDS Manual"
.SH NAME
zerodds-idlc \- read OMG IDL 4.2 and say what is in it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zerodds-idlc
.I SUBCOMMAND
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.I FILE.idl
\&...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads OMG IDL 4.2 and says what is in it: parses and lowers a file,
reports diagnostics, prints the canonical form or the model, and emits
target-language data-types plus DDS TypeSupport.
Implements the language PSMs (DDS-Java-PSM, DDS-PSM-Cxx, etc.).
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B check
Parse and lower; report every diagnostic.
.TP
.B lint
The rules a valid file can still break.
.TP
.B format
The canonical form, to stdout by default.
.TP
.B dump-ast
The model, as a tree.
.TP
.B print-deps
The types each declaration names.
.TP
.B generate
One language's source for the model.
.TP
.B dlrl
OMG DDS 1.2 \(sc8 DLRL object and home classes, from the three DLRL
pragmas.
.SS "dlrl and the DCPS_DATA_TYPE/DCPS_DATA_KEY collision"
DDS 1.2 \(sc8.2.2's Generation Process reads three pragmas out of the
IDL text:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#pragma DCPS_DATA_TYPE     "<scoped-name>"
#pragma DCPS_DATA_KEY      "<scoped-name> <field>"
#pragma DCPS_DLRL_RELATION "<scoped-name> <relation> <target>"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The first two spellings are not DLRL's alone: OpenDDS's own devguide
reuses the identical two pragma names for an unrelated, DLRL-free
topic-type/key marker, and
.B check
/
.B generate
already read them that way \(em the diagnostic on each now says so and
names the other reading.
.B dlrl
does not extend that AST-facet reading with a second, DLRL meaning for
the same two spellings; it reads the raw IDL text on its own, through
.IR zerodds_dlrl::pragma::parse_pragma ,
independently of
.IR idl\-lower .
Running
.B check
/
.B generate
and
.B dlrl
over the same file therefore each apply exactly one reading, and
neither silently eats the other's. A pragma that names one of the
three DLRL tags and does not parse under that grammar \(em wrong
arity, an unterminated quoted string \(em is reported as an error and
fails the run; a pragma outside the three tags is silent under
.BR dlrl ,
whatever it means to another tool.
.PP
The four backends emit classes that derive from PSM base types this
tree does not implement: C++'s from
.IR DLRL::ObjectRoot / ObjectHome / RefList
and
.IR DDS::Long
(DDS 1.2 \(sc B.6/\(sc B.7, the specification's own C++ PSM), Java's from
.I org.omg.dds.dlrl.ObjectRoot/ObjectHome
(\(sc B.8), C#'s from an
.I ObjectRoot/ObjectHome
pair and
.I [DlrlKey]/[DlrlRelation]
attributes this codebase invented for a target OMG never gave a PSM.
ZeroDDS ships a DLRL
.I runtime
for exactly one language, Rust, and
.B dlrl
does not target Rust \(em the four languages it does target have no
runtime here for the generated class to compile against, and every
generated file's header comment says so. TypeScript is the exception:
its generated interface and class name no external type at all.
.SS "generate and an IDL interface"
.B \-\-lang
.I rust
gives a full DDS-RPC 1.0 (formal/16-12-04) binding for every
.B interface
in the model: the request and reply topic types of \(sc7.5.1.1 with a
real XCDR2 codec, a typed
.IR <Iface>Client ,
a
.I <Iface>
service trait with
.B raises
in every method signature, and a
.I <Iface>Dispatcher
that serves it over
.BR zerodds\-rpc 's
.I Requester
/
.IR Replier .
An
.B out
or
.B inout
parameter is a
.I &mut
argument; a pure
.B out
parameter besides the declared return travels back through the
method's
.I Ok
value. A base interface's operations are reached through a Rust
supertrait
.RI ( "trait Derived: Base" )
and, on the client, through a composed base client field \(em
DDS-RPC 1.0 \(sc7.5.1.1.8 keeps a base's topic separate from a derived
interface's own. The generated file depends on
.IR zerodds\-rpc ", " zerodds\-dcps " and " zerodds\-wire ;
the preamble names them.
.PP
.IR c++ ", " java ", " typescript ", " csharp ", " go ", " swift ", " nim ", " zig ", " ada ", " d ", " elixir ", " julia ", " lua ", " ocaml " and " fsharp
also have complete
.B interface
bindings, each in the shape its own runtime and idiom gives it: every
operation and attribute reaches the output \(em an attribute as a
property, a
.IR name() / setName() \-shaped
pair, a
.IR get_ / set_
primitive operation or field, or a descriptor entry, whichever that
column already uses \(em and a declared
.BR raises / getraises / setraises
reaches it too. None has a codec, since none of those runtimes lives
in this tree; fourteen of the fifteen flatten a base interface's
members into the derived declaration, having no language mechanism to
compose a base through instead \(em Rust's supertrait, above, is the
one exception.
.PP
.IR c ", " python " and " node
have no mapping for an
.B interface
at all, and refuse a model that declares one \(em exit
.BR 1 ,
naming the interface, its position in the source, and which languages
do have a binding. Never a partial declaration, and never a silent
.B 0
with the interface missing from the output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-strict
OMG IDL 4.2 only; reject every vendor extension.
.TP
.B \-\-ros2
Admit the ROS 2 dialect: an unqualified member type that differs only in
case from the member name, such as
.BR "Pose pose;" ,
resolves to the type.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable diagnostics.
.TP
.B \-\-deny\-warnings
Exit non-zero when anything was reported.
.TP
.BR \-\-default\-extensibility " " \fIfinal\fR|\fIappendable\fR|\fImutable\fR
What an aggregate whose source said nothing is taken to be. Default:
the specification's
.IR appendable .
A source that did say still wins. Cyclone DDS's
.B idlc
takes an unannotated aggregate as
.IR final ,
RTI Connext, Fast DDS and OpenDDS as
.I appendable
(recorded in
.IR docs/wire-axis-flags.md );
for IDL written for Cyclone, pass
.BR "\-\-default\-extensibility final" .
.TP
.B \-\-check
.B format
only: name each non-canonical file, exit 1.
.TP
.B \-\-write
.B format
only: rewrite non-canonical files in place.
.TP
.BR \-\-lang " " \fINAME\fR
.B generate
/
.B dlrl
only, required: the language to emit \(em an unknown name lists the
valid ones for the subcommand it was given to.
.B generate
takes one of \fIrust\fR, \fIc++\fR, \fIc\fR, \fIjava\fR, \fIc#\fR, \fIpython\fR, \fItypescript\fR, \fIgo\fR, \fIocaml\fR, \fIada\fR, \fId\fR, \fIelixir\fR, \fIjulia\fR, \fIlua\fR, \fInim\fR, \fIswift\fR, \fIzig\fR, \fInode\fR, \fIada83\fR, \fIf#\fR, \fIkotlin\fR, or \fIall\fR for every one in one run.
.B dlrl
takes one of \fIc++\fR, \fIc#\fR, \fIjava\fR, \fItypescript\fR, or \fIall\fR for those four.
\fIcpp\fR/\fIcxx\fR, \fIcsharp\fR/\fIcs\fR, \fIfsharp\fR/\fIfs\fR and \fIts\fR are accepted besides, under either subcommand.
.TP
.BR \-\-java\-mapping " " \fIpsm\fR | \fIomg\fR
.B generate
only, with
.BR "\-\-lang java" " (or " "\-\-lang all" "):"
the shape of the generated Java types.
.I psm
(the default) is Clause 9 of DDS-Java-PSM 1.0: a structure, union, bit set and
exception is a final class with private state, a no-argument constructor and
Java Bean accessors, and an unbounded sequence is a
.BR java.util.Collection<E> ;
bounded sequences and arrays stay Java arrays.
.I omg
is the OMG IDL-to-Java mapping: public fields and a
.B T[]
for every sequence. The wire bytes are the same under both. Code that reads the
fields of the generated types directly passes
.BR "\-\-java\-mapping omg" .
.TP
.BR \-\-rpc\-mapping " " \fIbasic\fR | \fIenhanced\fR | \fIenhanced-empty\fR
.B generate
only: the service mapping of DDS-RPC 1.0 \(sc7.5.1 an
.B interface
is written for.
.I basic
(the default) is the Basic Service Mapping,
.I enhanced
the Enhanced Service Mapping, and
.I enhanced-empty
the Enhanced mapping with
.B @Empty
structures for an operation with nothing to carry.
.TP
.BR \-\-out " " \fIDIR\fR
.B generate
/
.B dlrl
only: where the file goes. Default: the current directory.
.TP
.BR \-I " " \fIDIR\fR ", " \-\-include " " \fIDIR\fR
Where a quoted
.B #include
is searched after the including file's own directory; repeatable, in the
order given.
.TP
.BR \-\-stamp " " \fIFILE\fR
.B generate
/
.B dlrl
only: written after every output of the run, and only when the run
succeeded \(em the one output a build system depends on.
.TP
.BR \-\-depfile " " \fIFILE\fR
.B generate
/
.B dlrl
only: a Make-format dependency file naming the stamp (or the manifest)
and every IDL file the run read, the
.BR #include d
ones included. Needs
.B \-\-stamp
or
.BR \-\-output\-manifest .
.TP
.BR \-\-output\-manifest " " \fIFILE\fR
.B generate
/
.B dlrl
only: JSON with the languages, the files read
.RB ( inputs )
and the files written
.RB ( outputs ).
.TP
.BR \-\-encoding " " \fIENC\fR
.B generate
only: one of
.IR xcdr1 ", " xcdr2 ", " pl\-cdr1 ", " pl\-cdr2 ", " delimited\-cdr .
Default:
.IR xcdr2 .
.TP
.BR \-\-endian " " \fIle\fR|\fIbe\fR
.B generate
only: the byte order the codec is planned for. It does not change the
emitted text \(em every wire runtime holds the order in its writer and
reader \(em but the preamble names it. Default:
.IR le .
.TP
.BR \-\-xcdr\-endianness " " \fIBE\fR|\fILE\fR
The DDS-TS 1.0 spelling of
.BR \-\-endian .
.TP
.BR \-\-cpp\-mapping " " \fIpsm\fR|\fInative\fR|\fIclassic\fR
.B generate
.RB "\-\-lang " c++
only: how topic types are spelled. Default:
.IR psm ,
the mapping of DDS\-PSM\-Cxx 1.0 \(sc7.4 and \(sc8.1 \(em classes with accessors,
.IR dds::core::optional ,
.IR dds::core::array ,
.IR std::map ,
.IR std::wstring ,
unions as classes with a discriminator \(em over the same codec: the structures the codec reads
and writes move into the namespace
.IR zd_native
and
.I zerodds::psm::Conv
converts on the way into the codec and out of it.
.I native
generates those structures under the names of the IDL: public fields, a plain pointer for
.BR @optional ,
.IR std::array ,
a map as a vector of pairs. The wire codec gates, the endpoint SDKs and every other consumer that
reads the fields of a generated header pass
.BR "\-\-cpp\-mapping native" .
.I classic
is the classic OMG IDL to C++ 1.3 mapping in C++03 over the same codec:
.IR CORBA::Long ,
.IR CORBA::String_var ,
.I zerodds::classic::Sequence
for a sequence and
.I zerodds::classic::Optional
for
.BR @optional .
.TP
.BR \-\-rust\-map " " \fIentries\fR|\fIbtree-map\fR
.B generate
.RB "\-\-lang " rust
only: how an IDL
.B map
is held.
.I entries
(the default) is the sequence of
.I MapEntry
structures XTypes 1.3 \(sc7.5.1.3.6 binds a map to;
.I btree-map
is a
.IR BTreeMap ,
which has the operations of a map and keeps its entries in ascending key order.
.TP
.BR \-\-dialect " " \fINAME\fR
.B generate
only: reproduce one peer's answers to every wire axis at once, before
any axis flag below overrides one of them. An unknown name lists the
valid ones. One of
.IR specification " (the default), " cyclone ", " rti ", " fastdds ", "
.IR opendds ", " opensplice ", " ros\-fastrtps ", " ros\-cyclone ", "
.IR omniorb ", " tao ", "
or
.IR jacorb .
The preamble comment names the dialect and every axis flag given.
See
.IR docs/wire\-axis\-flags.md
for what each dialect answers on each axis, and
.BR zerodds\-build 's
.B Config::dialect
for the same preset from a
.B build.rs .
.TP
.BR \-\-<axis> " " \fIVALUE\fR
.B generate
only: override one answer of the dialect (or of
.I specification
with no
.BR \-\-dialect ).
An unknown axis value lists the valid ones for that axis. Applied in
the order given, after
.BR \-\-dialect .
The axes, each with its flag and its values:
.RS
.TP
.B collection\-delimiters
specification, enum\-is\-primitive, never
.TP
.B default\-extensibility
final, appendable, mutable
.TP
.B defaulted\-discriminator\-member
type\-default, stream\-value
.TP
.B enum\-holder
declared, bit\-bound\-dropped
.TP
.B enumerator\-value
declared, ordinal
.TP
.B fixed\-digits
declared, value\-precision
.TP
.B key\-holder
forced\-final, as\-declared
.TP
.B key\-holder\-order
ascending\-member\-id, declaration
.TP
.B length\-codes
compact, reuse\-nested\-delimiter, short\-where\-it\-fits, universal\-nextint, sized\-or\-nextint
.TP
.B map\-delimiter
either\-half, value\-only
.TP
.B map\-entries
declaration, ascending\-key
.TP
.B map\-length
sequence\-equivalent, rule\-15\-omits
.TP
.B pid\-flags
sentinel\-must\-understand, bare, impl\-extension\-members
.TP
.B pl\-cdr1\-headers
specification, always\-extended, short\-true\-length
.TP
.B short\-pid\-range
up\-to\-reserved, all\-fourteen\-bits
.TP
.B union\-framing
specification, branches\-from\-zero
.TP
.B union\-labels
sign\-extended, negative\-unmatched\-below\-32
.TP
.B wchar\-framing
fixed, giop\-length\-prefixed, giop\-length\-prefixed\-member\-only
.TP
.B wchar\-width
2, 4
.TP
.B wstring\-length
specification, character\-count, character\-count\-terminated, specification\-wide\-units, ros\-type\-support, or the three coordinates
.IR counts / terminator / width
(octets|characters / unterminated|terminated / 2|4) for a cell none of the five names
.TP
.B wstring\-units
stream, big\-endian\-always, byte\-order\-mark, host\-unswapped
.RE
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
This text.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
The version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Nothing to report.
.TP
.B 1
A diagnostic was reported, or \fB\-\-check\fR found a file to reformat.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong.
.TP
.B 3
A file could not be read.
.SH FILES
.B generate
writes one file per input under
.BR \-\-out ,
named after the input's stem:
.IR <out>/<stem>.<ext>
(for example
.IR robot.rs ", " robot.hpp ", " robot.py );
C# adds
.IR <stem>.TypeSupport.cs .
Java and Ada write fixed names instead
.RI ( ZdTypes.java ", " ZdCodec.java ", " ZdTypeSupports.java ;
.IR zd_types.ads ", " zd_types.adb ).
Such a language refuses a second input with the same
.B \-\-out
in one run instead of overwriting the first; give each IDL file its own
.BR \-\-out ,
or
.B #include
them into one file.
.PP
.B dlrl
writes one bundled file per input for C++/C#/TypeScript, named
.IR <out>/<stem>_dlrl.<ext> ,
and one object file plus one home file per DLRL type for Java, named
.IR <out>/<Type>.java " and " <out>/<Type>Home.java .
The same second-input-same-name refusal applies.
